## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-preserving ML at scale with regulatory constraints, multi-tenant isolation, and production-grade governance. Requires knowledge of Vertex AI Confidential Computing, Dataflow pipelines, Data Catalog/DLP, and automated rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Vertex AI Confidential Computing (VMs/TPUs)
- Dataflow ingestion and residency controls
- Private endpoints and per-tenant budgets
- Data Catalog governance and DLP coverage
- Adversarial robustness testing and dataset/versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: basic adversarial test harness
function evalAdversarial(model, sample) {
  const adv = generateAdversarialSample(sample);
  const pred = model.predict(adv);
  return pred.accuracy;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to orchestrate per-hospital rollback across regions?
- How to quantify privacy risk and ensure end-to-end DLP coverage?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a modular, governance-minded pipeline that handles multilingual data and targeted evaluation without overcomplication. It emphasizes per-language data management, explainability, and rollback—common beginner-friendly MLOps concerns.

## Key Concepts
- Language-based routing and partitioning
- Per-language training subsets in Vertex AI
- Unified endpoint with traffic-splitting by language
- Explainable AI per language
- Drift monitoring and simple rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-configuration sketch for per-language training
const trainingPlan = {
  languages: ['en','es','de'],
  schedule: 'weekly',
  endpoint: {
    type: 'trafficSplit',
    rules: { en: 0.5, es: 0.3, de: 0.2 }
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add new languages with minimal downtime?
- How would you validate that explainability insights align with human judgments?
